#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162854} :activity/id #uuid "7a60cb12-dd77-5740-b1c2-9fd58434b948" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049174 :topic/id #uuid "e0ed8c88-7c9b-5941-8226-99cb93752f15" :topic/math-academy-id 3126} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162854 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163229 :tutorial/content "Consider a quadratic form \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a symmetric matrix. As we know, any symmetric matrix can be orthogonally diagonalized as follows:\n\n$$\nD = P^T \\! AP,\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ is an orthogonal matrix and $D$ is a diagonal matrix that contains eigenvalues of $A$ on the main diagonal. \n\nNow, substituting the change of variable $\\mathbf{x} = P \\mathbf{y}$ into our quadratic form, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =(Py)^{T}\\,A(Py) \\\\  & ={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}\\,AP)y \\\\  & ={y}^{T}\\,Dy,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives us a canonical form. \n\nAs a result, using the orthogonal diagonalization of $A,$ we can find the canonical form of $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x}.$\n\nFor example, let's find the canonical form of \n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2+4x_1x_2+x_2^2.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n![](images/43/43e0dc28161c0564a75f6ea2dc7f0e09ad74dffae05b864e13c61330221f2983.svg)\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, writing the eigenvalues in descending order, we obtain the diagonal matrix\n\n![](images/c9/c93cabd6a18df429b5aad99815b23e337c17b88a1c271dd17344631dfc5f8ebb.svg)\n\nThe corresponding quadratic form after a change of variables is\n\n$$\n3y_1^2 - y_2^2.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "26652f7d-7ad4-54a0-b4a6-5d9faafa8403" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4888 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6a819afd-fa45-5c12-8319-bbdf2452f54b" :step/math-academy-id 30542 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162892}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162892 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162005 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161797 :question/id #uuid "ce37b130-8aa9-58af-b3b8-bf78b5a741e7" :question/math-academy-id "e-8249"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a9a1aa0-8822-5dbd-8534-254ace55c555" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049450} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050001}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162630} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162631} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272180} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360530} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360531} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551351}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Canonical Form of a Quadratic Function by Finding Eigenvalues"} :step/id #uuid "1325a77d-0093-5d23-9495-014e8f550773" :step/math-academy-id 30849 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162893}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162893 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162006 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161798 :question/id #uuid "c8ec4ba0-e0bf-5842-8f5e-4914ce46706a" :question/math-academy-id "e-8250"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a1bee282-2775-59f5-a929-9dc5327703bc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049113}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162632} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162653} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360549} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360559} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364544} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400902} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404766}] :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Quadratic Function Over the 2D-Space to the Canonical Form Using Orthogonal Transformations"} :step/id #uuid "459f8fc0-d